  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1230 AM CDT Mon Sep 28 2026\n\nHot and mainly dry conditions will continue the first half of the \nwork week as mid-level ridging remains in control. The ridge begins \nto shift east Tuesday as a western CONUS trough approaches, \nallowing moisture and rain chances to increase from west to east. \nTemperatures should consequently begin trending downward by midweek \ndue to increased convection and cloud cover.\n\nAttention then turns to an increasing heavy rainfall and flash \nflooding threat Wednesday and Thursday. Deep Gulf moisture and \nPacific moisture from remnants of Polo will surge into South Texas \nahead of the approaching upper trough, with PWATs climbing above 2 \ninches and potentially approaching 2.5 inches Thursday. These values \nwould be more than 2 standard deviations above normal and support \nefficient rainfall production. Increasing large-scale ascent \ncombined with a developing pre-frontal trough, an embedded shortwave, \nand potentially a surface low should provide sufficient forcing for \nnumerous showers and thunderstorms. \n\nThe heavy rainfall threat initially increases across the Rio Grande \nPlains Wednesday, where WPC has portions of Webb county in a Slight \nRisk (level 2 of 4) for excessive rainfall. The threat becomes more \nwidespread Thursday as deeper moisture and stronger forcing overlap \nacross the region. WPC has placed all of South Texas in a Slight \nRisk for excessive rainfall Thursday. Locally heavy rainfall rates \nand repeated rounds of thunderstorms could result in flash flooding, \nparticularly where storms train or repeatedly move across the same \nlocations. Current QPF for Wednesday through Sunday generally ranges \nfrom 2.5 to 5 inches across South Texas. However, these amounts will \nlikely be refined over the coming days as confidence increases in \nthe placement and timing of the heaviest rainfall, especially as \nhigher-resolution guidance becomes available.\n\nUncertainty remains regarding the evolution of the mid-level low and \nhow far south the associated front ultimately progresses. However, a \ncomplete frontal passage does not appear necessary for heavy \nrainfall given the expected pre-frontal forcing and anomalously high \nmoisture. Rain chances remain high Thursday into Friday before \ngradually decreasing this weekend as the upper system shifts east.\n\nMinor coastal flooding also remains possible around times of high \ntide this week. P-ETSS guidance continues to indicate elevated water \nlevels following an upward trend, although relatively short swell \nperiods and modest onshore flow may limit the overall threat.",
